UNIT toPairs
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic

# Pure, read-only object-introspection utility. No seam (no clock/RNG/io/net), no
# argument mutation. Output is a deterministic function of the input's own-property
# structure and (for Map/Set) iteration order, both of which are fixed by ECMAScript.

SIG  toPairs(object) -> Array<[key, value]>
     # Single positional argument `object` (any value). Returns a fresh array of
     # two-element [key, value] pair-arrays. The argument is never mutated.

DEPENDS-ON none
     # Zero runtime dependencies. Self-contained. No carried-data table.

# =============================================================================
# BEHAVIOR  (ordered pipeline / BRANCH nodes — exact + complete)
# =============================================================================
#
# toPairs is `createToPairs(keys)` — i.e. a "type-tag dispatch" wrapped around the
# lodash `keys` collector. Evaluate the branches in THIS order:
#
# BRANCH-1  MAP:
#   If `object` is a Map (its internal Object.prototype.toString tag is
#   "[object Map]"), return its entries as [key, value] pairs in the Map's own
#   INSERTION order (Map.prototype.forEach order). Keys and values are taken
#   VERBATIM — a non-string key stays its original type (e.g. the number 3 stays
#   the number 3; an object key stays that object by reference). Empty Map -> [].
#     pair[i] = [key_i, value_i]  for the i-th entry in forEach order.
#
# BRANCH-2  SET:
#   Else if `object` is a Set (toString tag "[object Set]"), return one pair PER
#   MEMBER, each pair being [value, value] (the lodash Set convention — the member
#   is duplicated into both positions), in the Set's own INSERTION order
#   (Set.prototype.forEach order). Members are taken VERBATIM (a number member
#   stays a number). Empty Set -> [].
#     pair[i] = [member_i, member_i]
#
# BRANCH-3  EVERYTHING ELSE (the default / baseToPairs path):
#   Collect the key list K = keys(object)  (see KEY-COLLECTION below), then return
#   exactly  K.map(k => [k, object[k]]) , preserving K's order. Every key in K is a
#   STRING. The value is read with a plain property access `object[k]`; for a sparse
#   array hole this yields `undefined` (the pair is still emitted — see SPARSE).
#     pair[i] = [k_i, object[k_i]]
#
#   Note: WeakMap, Promise, DataView, RegExp, Date, plain objects, arrays, strings,
#   primitives, boxed primitives, typed arrays, arguments, array-like objects — ALL
#   fall into BRANCH-3 (only the exact Map and Set tags divert to 1/2).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KEY-COLLECTION  K = keys(object)   (the BRANCH-3 key list)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `keys` itself branches on array-likeness:
#
#   keys(object):
#     if isArrayLike(object):  K = arrayLikeKeys(object)
#     else:                    K = baseKeys(object)
#
#   isArrayLike(object) is TRUE iff:
#       object != null
#       AND typeof object.length == "number" with 0 <= length, length % 1 == 0,
#           length <= 9007199254740991 (MAX_SAFE_INTEGER)   [isLength]
#       AND object is NOT a function (toString tag not "[object Function]"/"[object GeneratorFunction]")
#   (Strings ARE array-like: a primitive string has a numeric .length. So is a real
#    Array, an arguments object, a typed array, and any plain object that happens to
#    carry a numeric `length`.)
#
#   -- arrayLikeKeys(value): used when isArrayLike is TRUE --
#     Step A: if value is a true Array (Array.isArray) OR an arguments object
#             (isArguments), seed result = ["0","1",...,String(value.length-1)]
#             (one string index per slot, INCLUDING sparse-hole slots), and set
#             skipIndexes = true.
#             Otherwise (e.g. a string primitive, a typed array, or a plain object
#             with a numeric length) seed result = [] and skipIndexes = false.
#       NOTE on strings & typed arrays: their per-index properties are OWN +
#       ENUMERABLE, so they are picked up by Step B's for-in even though Step A
#       did not seed them. Net effect: a string "abc" -> ["0","1","2"]; a
#       Uint8Array of len 3 -> ["0","1","2"].
#     Step B: for each key produced by a for-in enumeration of `value` (own +
#             inherited enumerable string keys, in JS for-in order):
#               include it IFF hasOwnProperty(value, key)
#                 AND NOT ( skipIndexes AND ( key == "length"
#                                             OR isIndex(key, result.length) ) )
#             i.e. when skipIndexes is true (real Array / arguments) the literal
#             "length" key and any canonical array-index key < length are NOT
#             re-added (they were already seeded / are structural); all OTHER own
#             enumerable keys (e.g. a named property "x" added onto an array) ARE
#             appended after the index keys.
#       isIndex(key, length): true iff key is a canonical unsigned-integer string
#             (regex ^(?:0|[1-9]\d*)$) or a number, with value > -1, integer, and
#             value < length.
#       CONSEQUENCE for a plain array-like object that is NOT a true Array/arguments
#       (skipIndexes = false): its own enumerable "length" and its index keys ARE
#       included, alongside all other own enumerable keys, in for-in order.
#       Example: {length:3, 0:"a", 1:"b", foo:"bar"} -> keys
#                ["0","1","length","foo"].
#
#   -- baseKeys(object): used when isArrayLike is FALSE --
#     if object is NOT a prototype object (isPrototype false):
#        K = Object.keys(Object(object))   # own ENUMERABLE string keys
#     else (object IS its constructor's .prototype, the rare prototype case):
#        K = for-in own enumerable string keys EXCEPT the literal key "constructor".
#     Object.keys order = ECMAScript OwnPropertyKeys order: integer-index keys
#     first in ASCENDING numeric order, then the remaining string keys in
#     property-INSERTION order. (Symbol keys are never included.)
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ORDERING (definitive, self-consistent with the generator)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * Plain object (BRANCH-3, baseKeys): integer-like keys ascending numerically,
#     THEN non-integer string keys in insertion order.
#       e.g. {z:1, a:2, "10":3, "2":4} -> keys ["2","10","z","a"].
#   * True Array / arguments: index keys "0".."length-1" in order, then any extra
#     own enumerable named keys in for-in order.
#   * String primitive / typed array: per-index "0".."length-1".
#   * Map / Set: own forEach (insertion) order.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EDGE / DEGENERATE CASES (all verified against the real package)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   * null        -> []   (isArrayLike false; baseKeys -> Object.keys path is not
#                          reached because... actually keys(null): isArrayLike(null)
#                          is false, baseKeys(null): isPrototype(null) false ->
#                          nativeKeys(null) = Object.keys(Object(null)) = [] ). NET: [].
#   * undefined   -> []   (same: Object.keys(Object(undefined)) == []).
#   * {}          -> []
#   * ""          -> []   (string of length 0 -> no index keys).
#   * number/boolean primitive (e.g. 42, true) -> []   (boxed, no own enum keys).
#   * new Number(5)  -> []   (Number wrapper has no own enumerable index props).
#   * new String("ab") -> [["0","a"],["1","b"]]  (String wrapper DOES expose own
#                          enumerable index props).
#   * RegExp /abc/g  -> []   (no own enumerable keys).
#   * empty Map -> []   ;   empty Set -> [].
#   * SPARSE arrays (see below) emit a pair for every slot, hole value = undefined.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPARSE  (load-bearing)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   For a true Array, the key list is "0".."length-1" REGARDLESS of holes
#   (seeded by Step A from value.length). The value for a hole index is read as
#   `object[k]` which is `undefined`. So  [1, <hole>, 3]  ->
#     [["0",1], ["1",undefined], ["2",3]]   (result length == array length == 3).
#   new Array(3) with [0]="a" -> [["0","a"], ["1",undefined], ["2",undefined]].
#   (Note: the held-out is captured via the real package, so the `undefined` second
#    element is part of the expected; a re-emitter that drops it diverges.)
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INCLUSION / EXCLUSION RULES (BRANCH-3)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   INCLUDED:  own + ENUMERABLE STRING-keyed properties.
#   EXCLUDED:  inherited/prototype properties (e.g. Foo.prototype.c when querying
#              new Foo); non-enumerable own properties; Symbol-keyed properties;
#              for true Array/arguments the structural "length" key; for a
#              prototype object the literal "constructor" key.
#     Verified: new Foo (this.a=1,this.b=2; proto.c=3) -> [["a",1],["b",2]].
#     Verified: {hidden non-enum, visible:1} -> [["visible",1]].
#     Verified: {[Symbol]:1, normal:2} -> [["normal",2]].
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VALUE FIDELITY
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   Values are passed THROUGH unchanged (identity preserved): object/array values
#   are the SAME references; -0 stays -0 (Object.is); NaN stays NaN; bigint stays
#   bigint. No coercion of values. Keys in BRANCH-3 are always strings; Map/Set
#   keys/members keep their original type.

# =============================================================================
ORACLE value -> oracle.json
# Pure deterministic value oracle: expected = real-package output for each emitted
# input. No seam injection needed (no ambient reads). Differential/quorum on the
# returned pair-array (deep structural equality, including the literal `undefined`
# hole values and key/value types).

# =============================================================================
SCOPE
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the entire pipeline above — the Map/Set
#   tag dispatch, the keys-collection rule (isArrayLike split, arrayLikeKeys with
#   skipIndexes, baseKeys with the prototype/constructor special-case), the own-
#   enumerable-string-key rule, the ECMAScript key ordering, sparse-hole emission,
#   the [k,v] vs [v,v] output shapes, and the empty/null/undefined/primitive
#   degenerate results.
# CARRIED DATA: none. (No literal tables; the only constants are MAX_SAFE_INTEGER
#   = 9007199254740991 and the uint-index regex /^(?:0|[1-9]\d*)$/, both stated
#   inline above — they are logic constants, not a carried table.)
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT: none. The unit performs no mutation, no side effect, no ambient
#   read. MUTATION-SAFETY: N/A — toPairs is read-only and never writes to its
#   argument.
# OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs because they are not
#   ORACLE-CODEC-faithful, yet a blind re-emitter still reconstructs them from the
#   spec above): (a) function values as the argument -> toPairs returns [] for a
#   bare function (isFunction true so isArrayLike false; baseKeys -> Object.keys of
#   a function = its own enum string keys, usually none -> []); (b) Symbol-keyed
#   properties -> always excluded; (c) raw `undefined` values reachable only via
#   sparse holes ARE in-envelope (the array container round-trips and the hole is
#   recreated by the codec); we DO emit those.

# =============================================================================
# EMISSION CONTRACT (for the blind re-emitter — READ THIS)
# =============================================================================
# The unit is the package's SINGLE DEFAULT export (CommonJS `module.exports = fn`).
# The re-emitter MUST emit an ES module whose DEFAULT export is the single function
# value:
#       export default function toPairs(object) { ... }
#   (or `const toPairs = ...; export default toPairs;`)
# Exactly ONE function value is exported as the default. No named exports are
# required. The function takes one positional argument and returns the pair array
# as specified. It must NOT mutate the argument. It must NOT require('lodash') or
# any dependency — reconstruct the logic inline.

# =============================================================================
PROVENANCE
